            <ArticleTitle>Idiopathic localized hydromyelia: dilatation of the central canal of the spinal cord of probable congenital origin.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>351-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Three adult patients are reported with asymptomatic localized widening of the central canal of the spinal cord. These patients were followed for a period of 24 years by imaging and/or clinical history and physical examination without evidence of signs or symptoms related to the spinal cord. This condition probably represents persistence into adult life of a fetal configuration of the central canal of the spinal cord. This process may be termed &quot;idiopathic localized hydromyelia&quot; to distinguish it from syringomyelia secondary to such causes as Chiari malformation, trauma, infection, or neoplasm.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
